# Let Git decide text vs binary, and normalise text to LF *in the repository*.
# This does not change what Windows checks out — core.autocrlf still governs the
# working tree — it only guarantees the committed blob is LF.
* text=auto

# ── Files that are EXECUTED on the Linux VPS ────────────────────────────────
# These must be LF in the WORKING TREE too, not just in the repository, because
# they are copied to the server verbatim (scp, docker cp, docker build context)
# rather than checked out there. A CRLF shebang makes the kernel look for an
# interpreter literally named "/bin/bash\r" and fails with
#   bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# The same class of bug bit docker-compose.yml on 2026-09-24: a CRLF copy was
# scp'd to the server, and `sed -i 's/^    cap_add:$/.../'` then matched nothing
# because of the trailing \r. sed reported success, the edit silently did not
# happen, and Piston stayed down. eol=lf on the working tree prevents that at
# source.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.env.example text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# ── Deliberately NOT pinned: application source ─────────────────────────────
# *.ts, *.tsx, *.js and *.json are intentionally left to core.autocrlf (which is
# `true` on the Windows dev machines here). They are compiled, bundled or read by
# Node — never executed as a shell script and never copied to the server raw — so
# CRLF in the working tree is harmless. Pinning them to eol=lf would rewrite
# thousands of working-tree files on the next checkout for every Windows
# developer, producing a large and risky diff for no operational benefit.
#
# Add a rule here only for a file that is copied to a Linux host verbatim.

# ── Binaries: never normalise ───────────────────────────────────────────────
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.xlsx binary
